  "Question": "The side lengths \\(a,b,c\\) of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) satisfy \\((a+b)^2-c^2=2ab\\). What type of triangle is it?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Acute triangle",
    "B": "Right triangle",
    "C": "Obtuse triangle",
    "D": "Equilateral triangle"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Expanding gives \\(a^2+2ab+b^2-c^2=2ab\\), so \\(a^2+b^2=c^2\\). By the converse of the Pythagorean theorem, it is a right triangle.",
